WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.600
Art is an elusive specter.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:10.340
It can be harder to catch than SCP-682, harder
to explain than SCP-055, and have as profound

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.940
effects on our minds as SCP-035.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.450
It exists in our hearts and our brains.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.850
It’s something we can both discover and
impose.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:26.750
Paintings, sculptures, performance, music,
film, photography, animation, game design,

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.560
and so much more exists under its umbrella.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:34.730
Thomas Merton once said, “Art enables us
to find ourselves and lose ourselves at the

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:35.840
same time.”

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.390
And we here at SCP Explained are inclined
to agree.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:43.370
Even the video you’re watching right now
is a kind of art: It’s the product of hard

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:49.150
work by writers, editors, animators, a voice
artist, and the producers who bring it all

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:50.150
together.

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:51.890
Really, where would we be without it?

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:56.080
But of course, there’s always going to be
people out there who want to push the boundaries

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.580
of art just that little bit further.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:03.070
The avant-garde artists who work on the fringes,
pioneers willing to delve further into the

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:07.590
darkest corners of human experience - And
sometimes even beyond.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:13.780
That’s right, welcome to the wonderful and
weird world of Anartists - A word that, in

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.460
and of itself, has a kind of artistry to it.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:22.380
It’s short for Anomalous Artists - As in,
artists who work with the medium of explicitly

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.890
anomalous objects or materials.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:30.260
The more perceptive among you may also have
noticed its similarity to the word “Anarchists”,

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:36.060
as in, rejectors of rigid rules and order
- like the kind imposed by the SCP Foundation.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.270
And when it comes to the world of Anartists,
you’ll find that a certain group comes up

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:44.170
again and again and again: Are We Cool Yet?

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:48.450
You’ve probably heard it before in passing,
but there’s a lot about this anomalous art

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.810
collective that remains mysterious…

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:51.890
Until today.

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:57.920
We’re going to grab our berets and paint-stained
aprons and dive deep into the Anart scene,

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.920
as well as their most controversial group
of interest.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:06.310
One thing is for sure: It’s going to get
incredibly, incredibly strange from here on

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:07.310
in.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:10.119
But hey, it’s art, what can you expect?

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:12.519
The structure and reputation of Are We Cool
Yet?

00:02:12.519 --> 00:02:17.139
Is a complicated and contentious topic even
within the world of Anart.

00:02:17.139 --> 00:02:21.669
Much like the Serpent’s Hand, the group
is incredibly decentralized, with no governing

00:02:21.669 --> 00:02:26.200
body or binding ethos, save for the commitment
to producing Anart.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:27.770
Proponents of Are We Cool Yet?

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:30.329
think of it as a kind of binding ethos.

00:02:30.329 --> 00:02:32.859
Someone can become a member of Are We Cool
Yet?

00:02:32.859 --> 00:02:35.499
Without even knowing about the group beforehand.

00:02:35.499 --> 00:02:41.120
This is why, as long as the memory and reputation
of the group remains alive, the SCP Foundation

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.310
can’t effectively shut them down.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:45.760
Some work under the banner of Are We Cool
Yet?

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.510
Alone, toiling away, driven by sheer passion
for the art they create.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:55.489
Others work in creative salons, typically
led by a smaller group of artistic visionaries,

00:02:55.489 --> 00:02:59.799
working to turn their combined skills into
more ambitious varieties of Anart.

00:02:59.799 --> 00:03:04.819
A common philosophy of the group is wanting
to make their art visible to the world, often

00:03:04.819 --> 00:03:07.879
to make a statement or start a discussion
among the public.

00:03:07.879 --> 00:03:12.329
Naturally, this particular drive is liable
to make local Foundation authorities burst

00:03:12.329 --> 00:03:13.530
a blood vessel.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.340
Or maybe they really don’t just get art?

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.819
I mean, look at Dr. Gears, especially.

00:03:17.819 --> 00:03:21.390
Does he strike you as the kind of guy who
would take some time out of his day to stroll

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:22.400
around a gallery?

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.439
He’d probably murmur some snide comment
about it being a waste of printing paper.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:27.439
Ahem.

00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:31.169
Anyway, before we proceed any further into
Are We Cool Yet?

00:03:31.169 --> 00:03:35.780
As it is today, its relationship with other
groups of interest, its splinter cells, and

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:40.709
some of the notable creations its members
have produced, we need to go back to the past

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:42.709
and discuss the group’s creation.

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:47.439
Thankfully, this group’s history can be
traced back to the 1800s rather than us needing

00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:51.530
to trace it all the way back to the creation
of humanity and the universe, like the Church

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.840
of the Broken God or the Children of the Scarlet
King.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.269
The rather amazing thing about this particular
group is that its whole identity can be traced

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:05.699
back to an argument between two famous artists:
Marcel Duchamp and Ruiz Marcos.

00:04:05.699 --> 00:04:09.849
For those who need a little refresher on art
history, Duchamp’s most famous pieces are

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:13.180
probably Fountain, the iconic signed urinal
and

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:19.200
L.H.O.O.Q., the painting of the Mona Lisa
defaced with a mustache and goatee.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.830
And Ruiz Marcos is best known for painting
the colors in your darkest nightmares, that

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:27.800
strange feeling you get at an empty train
station in the middle of the night, and the

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.670
subtle whiff of burnt toast you get before
a cerebral event.

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:37.700
The following is a critcal extract from The
Coolest War: Memories of a Critic, an anonymously-penned

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:43.680
opus on the nature of Anart and the creation
of Are We Cool Yet?, detailing the group’s

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.030
early history and defining embryonic moments.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:50.520
“In the nineteenth century, the march of
progress made it possible for the first time

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:56.480
for human science to contemplate and comprehend
those phenomena which had long seemed to contravene

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:58.020
the laws of nature.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:02.180
Within a matter of decades, concepts that
had long been viewed as witchcraft or sorcery

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:06.970
were laid bare in the terms of a new and secret
science, and governments and organizations

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:11.350
around the world began to classify, study,
and collect all such anomalies.

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:15.191
And, just as artists throughout history have
turned a critical eye to the events of the

00:05:15.191 --> 00:05:20.070
day in their work, so too did the artists
of this scientific Renaissance begin to interpret

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:22.380
these new discoveries on canvas.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:27.200
By the 1870s, Paris was the center of the
world of anomalous art, and the city stood

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.560
witness to endless debates about the role
of the anomalous in art, or whether such a

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.340
role existed at all.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:39.560
When in 1874 the famed Salon des Magnifiques
refused to allow any "works of a phantasmagorical

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:44.800
nature" to be displayed at their grand exhibition,
those artists shut out by the committee organized

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.310
their own counter-exhibition, to be held at
the same time across the river.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:53.911
"Sommes-Nous Devenus Magnifiques?", as the
show came to be called, was the talk of the

00:05:53.911 --> 00:05:59.790
Parisian press for months, earning equal amounts
of curiosity, dismissal, and outright derision,

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:05.170
but the exiled gadflies had made their point
- the paranormal, the anomalous, and the bizarre

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:10.030
had found a place in the world of art and
would not be so easily gotten rid of.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:11.640
"Sommes-Nous Devenus Magnifiques?"

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.440
held its exhibitions every ten years thereafter,
and as time passed the world of anomalous

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:17.830
art grew larger.

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:21.720
From its beginnings in Paris, artists from
all across France and Europe, and later from

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.210
the Americas and the Orient as well, began
attending the increasingly prestigious - and

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:31.280
increasingly bizarre and difficult to keep
hidden from the disdainful eyes of a concerned

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.250
government - exhibition, expressing new and
different interpretations of the role of the

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.200
anomalous in human life.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:42.900
It was at the sixth decennial exhibition in
1924 that the growing rift between the two

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:49.470
largest schools of thought - logical vs. emotional,
science vs. faith, Old World vs. New - came

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:54.670
to a head, for it was at that show that for
the first time, the works of the French surrealist

00:06:54.670 --> 00:07:00.330
Marcel Duchamp would be exhibited alongside
those of Ruiz Marcos, the Mexican artist whose

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:04.850
themes of magical realism and religious awe
intertwined with the viscerally accessible

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:09.730
Forteana of our world had set art critics
worldwide at war with each other.

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:13.810
Those who saw the two during the days leading
up to the opening of the exhibition said they

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:18.790
spent nearly the entire time in heated discussion
with one another (in English, for neither

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:23.900
spoke the other's mother tongue confidently)
about everything under the Sun - the importance

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:28.650
of the artist in relation to his work, the
importance of context, faith, knowledge, law,

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:33.910
free will, God, the State, democracy, Marxism,
the war, the League of Nations, and the best

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:36.050
way to serve a cup of coffee.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:40.010
It seemed that they might continue bickering
throughout the entire exhibition, but as the

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:43.491
artists prepared to greet the assembled press
on the morning of its grand opening, they

00:07:43.491 --> 00:07:46.960
appeared to have eventually come to an understanding.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:51.510
If there is one image that comes to the mind
of any art historian when the 1924 Expo is

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:56.640
brought up, it is of that iconic photo of
Duchamp and Ruiz posing side by side with

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.780
their fellow artists in front of the still-closed
doors, Marcos seemingly leaning over to whisper

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:03.430
something into Duchamp's ear.

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:07.620
For decades, many have speculated on what
words Marcos had for his colleague during

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.810
that memorable instant; a question of metaphysics?

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:11.810
Or a challenge?

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:13.780
An affirmation of their coming to terms?

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:18.260
A reminder of the reason why they were there
at that moment of time?

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:22.220
Perhaps an expression of amazement at the
multitudes that had come to see them?

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:26.480
According to one reporter who claimed he stood
close enough to overhear that whisper amidst

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:32.110
the din of the crowd, it was all five at once,
expressed in four simple words;

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:34.560
"Are we cool yet?"”

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:37.919
As you can see, the themes that run through
the entirety of this group of interest got

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:39.550
their starts early on.

00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:43.330
The leading ones are conflict and the desire
to prove oneself.

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:48.510
Being excluded from the prestigious 19th-century
French art salon, Salon des Magnifiques, led

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:53.700
to them creating their iconic gathering and
exhibition: Sommes-Nous Devenus Magnifiques?,

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:57.019
which translates loosely to “Have We Become
Magnificent?”

00:08:57.019 --> 00:09:00.129
- An ironic jab about gatekeeping in the art
world.

00:09:00.129 --> 00:09:04.870
To have one’s Anart featured at Sommes-Nous
Devenus Magnifiques?, held only once every

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:10.339
ten years, is the greatest honor for any artist
that is a devotee of Are We Cool Yet?

00:09:10.339 --> 00:09:11.540
And their practices.

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:15.420
However, something that is extremely important
to note is that this doesn’t necessarily

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:17.949
apply to all Anartists out there.

00:09:17.949 --> 00:09:23.509
After all, Anartists, like all creative spirits,
have a tendency to be highly individualistic,

00:09:23.509 --> 00:09:26.749
and some have bones to pick with the way that
Are We Cool Yet?

00:09:26.749 --> 00:09:27.749
Operates.

00:09:27.749 --> 00:09:32.470
One notable splinter group broke away in 2013,
to form a far smaller group of interest known

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:34.870
as Gamers Against Weed.

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:38.399
The Gamers didn’t exactly jive with the
fact that some of the Anart made by members

00:09:38.399 --> 00:09:39.760
of Are We Cool Yet?

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:40.760
Hurt people.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:45.639
Specifically, the founder of Gamers Against
Weed, Jude Kriyot, lost a friend to a dangerous

00:09:45.639 --> 00:09:48.160
piece of Anart at an Are We Cool Yet?

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:49.160
Exhibition.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:51.209
The Gamers also just found that Are We Cool
Yet?

00:09:51.209 --> 00:09:55.670
Had a weirdly intense energy that seriously
harshed their vibe, and as a result, they

00:09:55.670 --> 00:10:00.370
went in their own direction to perform more
harmless and goofy anomalous pranks - Like

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:07.180
creating SCP-3108, the Nerfing Gun, and SCP-2586,
Mr. Literal Serial Killer.

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:10.009
And we swear that’s less nasty than it sounds!

00:10:10.009 --> 00:10:14.449
The violence that this particular art collective
allows is a point of contention among a lot

00:10:14.449 --> 00:10:15.890
of Anartists.

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:20.070
Some ex-members have joined the more pacifistic
sects of the Serpent’s Hand, where they

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:23.910
can still follow their dreams of exposing
the anomalous to the public, without needing

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:28.399
to rub elbows with the kind of people who
believe that art projects have a certain amount

00:10:28.399 --> 00:10:30.990
of “acceptable casualties.”

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:34.730
Naturally, because of their laissez-faire
attitude towards death and destruction as

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:39.120
well as their commitment to making sure their
unique Anart is seen by the world, Are We

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:40.120
Cool Yet?

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:45.910
Is actively despised by The SCP Foundation,
the Global Occult Coalition, the FBI’s Unusual

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:49.209
Incidents Unit, and the Horizon Initiative.

00:10:49.209 --> 00:10:53.829
Confirmed members are to be arrested, contained,
and questioned for further information on

00:10:53.829 --> 00:10:54.829
sight.

00:10:54.829 --> 00:10:58.550
Dr. Wondertainment is a lot more amicable
with members of Are We Cool Yet?

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:02.490
After all, a lot of artists aren’t quite
able to pay their bills on their passion projects

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:07.749
alone, and as much as the “starving artist”
is a romanticized concept, these people do

00:11:07.749 --> 00:11:09.749
still really need to eat.

00:11:09.749 --> 00:11:14.420
So there are definitely worse things an aspiring
Anartist can do than take a part-time design

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:18.130
job with the fine folks at the Dr. Wondertainment
Toy Company.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:23.829
They utilize the inherent creativity and anomaly-making
skills of these desperate Anartists to create

00:11:23.829 --> 00:11:26.279
their most delightfully devilish toys yet.

00:11:26.279 --> 00:11:31.459
And there’s no shame in that - After all,
Salvador Dali did some work with Disney and

00:11:31.459 --> 00:11:33.149
designed candy logos.

00:11:33.149 --> 00:11:35.550
We all have our side gigs!

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:37.149
Members and cells of Are We Cool Yet?

00:11:37.149 --> 00:11:40.759
Have also had documented links to some of
the more powerful and dangerous groups of

00:11:40.759 --> 00:11:42.129
interest out there.

00:11:42.129 --> 00:11:47.290
On the more mild side of this new spectrum,
there’s HANSARP, a German dadaist and nihilist

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:50.310
group that was formed by members of Are We
Cool Yet?

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:55.069
Under the instructions of the Church of the
Broken God-affiliated leader, Boris Aldermann.

00:11:55.069 --> 00:11:57.850
We may need to give a little extra context
here.

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:03.369
Dada or Dadaism, to give a brief and highly
reductive definition, is an early-20th century

00:12:03.369 --> 00:12:07.949
art movement formed in the wake of the horrors
of World War One, which highly disapproved

00:12:07.949 --> 00:12:10.569
of war, violence, and nationalism.

00:12:10.569 --> 00:12:14.249
It sought to challenge the very boundaries
of art and what could really be considered

00:12:14.249 --> 00:12:18.829
“art” in the first place - so naturally,
it has a lot of common philosophical ground

00:12:18.829 --> 00:12:21.850
with the loose central ethos of the Are We
Cool Yet?

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:22.850
Movement.

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:27.679
And Nihilism is, in short, the belief in the
lack of any inherent meaning in life.

00:12:27.679 --> 00:12:31.800
So it goes without saying that HANSARP are
some pretty intense folks.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:37.170
But not nearly intense as Grand Karcist Ion
or the Scarlet King, and cells of Are We Cool

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:41.379
Yet? have been associated with both the Sarkicists
and the Scarlet King worshippers.

00:12:41.379 --> 00:12:45.379
However, their relationships with some of
the Sarkicists they’ve dealt with have been

00:12:45.379 --> 00:12:47.179
frosty, to say the least.

00:12:47.179 --> 00:12:53.620
For example, in SCP-2714, an anomalous copy
of the Adventures of Tintin graphic novel,

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:54.620
members of Are We Cool Yet?

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:59.139
tricked a Sarkicist into trapping one of his
holy figures in the comic book, before killing

00:12:59.139 --> 00:13:00.550
the poor sap’s father.

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:05.089
A lot of creators suffer for their art, but
the really unpleasant folks make other people

00:13:05.089 --> 00:13:09.300
suffer for their art, and Are We Cool Yet?
has plenty of those.

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:10.300
Another Are We Cool Yet?

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:15.369
cell known as Cool Yet Radio, which focused
on making audio-based anomalous art pieces,

00:13:15.369 --> 00:13:21.800
created SCP-4150, a version of the classic
Beatles song Penny Lane with a cognitohazardous

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.019
effect added.

00:13:23.019 --> 00:13:27.990
Intriguingly, this song contains a lyric referencing
the Seventh King, which is believed to be

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:33.990
a nod to - you guessed it - the ever-unpleasant
nightmarish chaos god, The Scarlet King.

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:38.959
There are truly all kinds of members of Are
We Cool Yet?, covering an extremely wide spectrum

00:13:38.959 --> 00:13:40.029
of beliefs.

00:13:40.029 --> 00:13:42.980
But I think we’ve heard more than enough
about the artists for now.

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:47.970
How about instead, we have our own little
Sommes-Nous Devenus Magnifiques?

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:51.970
Exhibition right here, and take a look at
some of Are We Cool Yet?’s finest pieces

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:53.369
of anomalous art.

00:13:53.369 --> 00:13:58.449
Of course, the non-anomalous world is no stranger
to dangerous and provocative art pieces.

00:13:58.449 --> 00:14:03.329
Performance artist Marina Abramović put herself
in real danger by involving knives and guns

00:14:03.329 --> 00:14:08.429
in her performance piece Rhythm Zero, and
Ron Athey put himself at the mercy of a wig

00:14:08.429 --> 00:14:12.569
full of needles to raise awareness of HIV
in his piece Self-Obliteration.

00:14:12.569 --> 00:14:19.040
But no non-anomalous art piece will ever be
as deadly as SCP-173.

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:26.500
That’s right: The Sculpture is heavily theorized
to be a piece of deadly Anart created by a

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.369
powerful Anartist known as The Sculptor.

00:14:29.369 --> 00:14:33.950
It’s believed to be a commentary on the
nature of humanity itself: An attention seeker

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:37.029
who only knows how to commit violence and
defecate.

00:14:37.029 --> 00:14:44.079
For something a little less deadly, there’s
SCP-092, a collection of 3125 CDs each titled

00:14:44.079 --> 00:14:48.910
“The Absolute Absolute Absolute Absolute
BEST of The 5th Dimension!!!!!”

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:53.370
This piece was created collaboratively by
the musical group 5th Dimension and a member

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:55.069
of Are We Cool Yet?

00:14:55.069 --> 00:14:59.170
Anyone listening to the album is forced to
listen to every song in a row, at which point

00:14:59.170 --> 00:15:02.649
anyone who listened will exclaim “Wow, that
was real cool.”

00:15:02.649 --> 00:15:07.480
However, the artist felt this wasn’t their
finest work, and turned themself and the CDs

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:10.290
in to the SCP Foundation for voluntary containment.

00:15:10.290 --> 00:15:15.490
Then there’s the commissioned piece SCP-2635,
the hot potato.

00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:20.279
This was initially paid for by a Chechen Rebel
Group planning to take out their Russian enemies,

00:15:20.279 --> 00:15:24.720
and the Anartist taking the commission was
happy to give it a theatrical and artistic

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:25.720
flair.

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:29.670
Any group that possesses the potato for more
than one calendar year will lose half of its

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:33.279
members, as they begin to spontaneously combust.

00:15:33.279 --> 00:15:37.379
To keep these effects from occurring, it’s
continually passed between groups, living

00:15:37.379 --> 00:15:40.199
up to its nickname as the hot potato.

00:15:40.199 --> 00:15:41.899
Some great art can come out of spite.

00:15:41.899 --> 00:15:47.939
A good example is SCP-4879, a soapstone polar
bear statue that has the frightening ability

00:15:47.939 --> 00:15:52.179
to take away the special talents of anyone
standing in its proximity.

00:15:52.179 --> 00:15:56.579
It was created by an Anartist filled with
spite at the success and talent of his fellow

00:15:56.579 --> 00:15:59.939
artists and wished to take away their artistic
spark.

00:15:59.939 --> 00:16:04.459
However, in what seems like a meditation on
the ultimately self-destructive nature of

00:16:04.459 --> 00:16:10.699
spite, the very creation of SCP-4879 wound
up forever squashing whatever creative abilities

00:16:10.699 --> 00:16:12.239
its creator had.

00:16:12.239 --> 00:16:18.560
Then we have this little anomalous art taster
plate, the nightmare-inducing SCP-1057, a

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:23.740
kind of anti-shadow, a selectively tangible
shape that, despite technically not being

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:27.040
there, can behave and kill like an Adult Tiger
Shark.

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:31.250
And to make things even more frightening,
this beast was first found after it devoured

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:33.910
people in an otherwise normal swimming pool.

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:38.299
A note left by the antishark’s creator on
the swimming pool’s bulletin board read,

00:16:38.299 --> 00:16:43.220
“Panic means that the idea of a shark can
be more dangerous than an actual shark.

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:46.069
It can be even more dangerous than no shark
at all.

00:16:46.069 --> 00:16:47.619
Are we cool yet?”

00:16:47.619 --> 00:16:49.499
And we’re sure that means…

00:16:49.499 --> 00:16:50.499
Something.

00:16:50.499 --> 00:16:55.429
There you have it, folks, our crash course
on the SCP Foundation’s most artistic enemy,

00:16:55.429 --> 00:16:57.429
the Are We Cool Yet Collective?

00:16:57.429 --> 00:17:01.319
And look, we’re not saying that the Chaos
Insurgency are nice people, but they at least

00:17:01.319 --> 00:17:05.770
have the decency to not tell us we don’t
like their murderous site raids because we

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:07.760
“just don’t get it, man.”

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:13.630
Now go check out “SCP-001 Ouroboros Cycle
- The Full Story Compilation” and “SCP

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:18.340
Foundation Boss - The Administrator Explained”
for more from the weird, wild world of the

00:17:18.340 --> 00:17:19.430
SCP Foundation!

